fix(collect): stop the degraded paths from lying or over-fetching (#885)

Review of #887 found three gaps, all in the paths that run when something
has already gone wrong.

`trace_status` replaces `trace_ok` with three states. `missing` and `short`
are populated only by the post-copy verification loop, and an inventory-probe
failure returns before that loop — so both were empty because nothing had
been examined, and the report cheerfully printed "trace_data.csv is present
and complete — the workload's trace is safe" beside "transferred 0 of 0
files" and the probe error. That is the same false reassurance the issue was
filed about, one step earlier in the pipeline. An `IterationCopy` now records
whether verification ran, and an unverified record reports UNKNOWN.

The refetch decision now requires positive evidence the remote moved on.
`stale_marker` was a bare presence test, so a failed mtime probe — which
blocks the skip in `_is_iteration_up_to_date` and therefore routes every
iteration into the copy — was read as "remote advanced" and refetched the
entire inventory. That re-exposed the ~420 MB ceiling in precisely the
degradation path the README claims is cheap. The comparison lives in one
`_marker_covers` helper the gate and the copy share, so the two cannot drift
about what stale means; a marker whose mtime is absent or unparseable no
longer forces a refetch (absent) or is trusted (unparseable).

An unparseable `stat` line now fails the whole inventory instead of being
dropped with a warning. A dropped entry can never appear in `missing` or
`short`, so the iteration could be marked complete and skipped forever while
that file was never fetched — the same permanent data loss as defect 3, moved
down into the parser. This one was raised by a reviewer lens and not carried
into the verdict; fixed anyway, because the failure mode is the one this
issue exists to close and the fix is three lines.

Also corrects the `sim2real-analyze` layout note, which said an absent marker
means the iteration was copied partially. It means completeness is unproven —
true for every run collected before this change and for any iteration never
collected at all.

